<tal:root define="dummy nothing"
>data-sitelayer-override=mail_password_template

This text comes from a site package jbot directory, registered on a browser
layer that extends IEmailkitLayer. If you are reading it in a test assertion,
SPEC 8.2 level 1 holds.

The site name below is read from the flat render() context, so a render proves
the override was really executed and not merely resolved on disk. It is also
the point of the change that made this file possible: the template a site
overrides is now an ordinary one in the ordinary dialect, so a site author
writes ${site_name}, not python:options['member'].getProperty(...).

Site name : <span tal:omit-tag="" tal:content="site_name" />
Reset link : <span tal:omit-tag="" tal:content="reset_url" />

Three authoring notes, all learned the hard way while writing this file:
a tal: element accepts TAL attributes only, so the marker above is a text line
rather than an attribute; prose in a template must never contain angle brackets,
because a tag name mentioned in passing is parsed as real markup and silently
reshapes the document; and the root element below must be closed, since
Chameleon accepts an unclosed one without complaint but the enclosing define
scope collapses and every name in it reads as undefined at render time.
</tal:root>
